page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Colour — from inside the museum</title><style>'''+css+'''</style><main><p>Ada Research · 24 September 2026</p><h1>Colour, from inside the museum</h1><p>Small reading changes across the eight halls, with photographs taken from standing positions in the museum. The columns, furniture and surrounding architecture remain in view.</p><nav><a href="#reading">Text differences</a><a href="/compose/sources?map=Color_Context_Placed&amp;doc=final">Read Colour’s opening</a><a href="/book-tasks/review?seq=color">Review queue</a><a href="DECISIONS.md">Decisions and evidence</a></nav><figure><img src="colour-hall.png" alt="Inside the museum’s long Colour hall, among stacks of coloured primitives"><figcaption>Entering the column hall. The familiar primitives stand around a route you can walk.</figcaption></figure><h2>Saved choices, taller relatives</h2><figure><img src="among-the-columns.png" alt="A standing view among tall striped and blue columns inside the museum"><figcaption>The taller studies at the far end. The striped column’s plate now describes its change from table scale to architectural height.</figcaption></figure><p>The catalogue of names is shorter. The bridge to DNA and form grammar remains: a saved composition can be read again, enlarged or combined with another. The chapter still asks what the visitor can notice before explaining how colour is made.</p><h2>The choosing hand</h2><div class="pair"><figure><img src="salon-red.png" alt="The giant and petite hands with red nails in the museum salon"><figcaption>One nail choice reaches both display hands.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="salon-violet.png" alt="The same salon view with violet nails and unchanged skin colour"><figcaption>Change the nails; the skin, furniture and viewpoint stay.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The text now stays with that encounter. Separate native checks confirm that the same choice reaches the production left and right hand-material receivers. Those receivers were invisible fixtures for this run; these are display-hand photographs, not tracked headset captures.</p><h2>What changed</h2><p>Eleven review comments resolved. Six manuscripts received focused edits; Flashlight and Pillar prose remain intact. Added artistic attribution, clarified the rainbow’s optional entrance objects, corrected a gradient description and aligned the striped-column plate with the prose. Four mistaken score-display tokens now name the authored standalone spawn cells.</p><p><a href="columns/report.json">12 column checks</a> · <a href="nails/report.json">59 salon checks</a> · <a href="array-museum/report.json">27 Array checks</a>. All runs exited cleanly. Headset movement and learner experience remain untested; no live bake/save was replaced.</p><details><summary>Array: the museum currently contains this wireframe room</summary><img src="museum-approach.png" alt="Looking into the wireframe Array room from the museum passage"><p>New entrance and standing views replace the tight book and review photographs. The room’s current wireframe materials remain unchanged. <a href="../2026-09-24-arrays-review-comments/index.html">See the updated Array comparison</a>.</p></details><h2 id="reading">Exact changes in final.md</h2>'''+''.join(comparisons)+'''<h2>Comment by comment</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Hall</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+''.join(rows)+'''</tbody></table></main></html>'''
